  <h1>Quality Control Requirements</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Quality control in rapid-test manufacturing means lot-level validation, internal control lines, and ISO 13485 traceability from raw material to finished device.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>QC element</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Internal control line</th><td>Built into every device — confirms the test ran correctly</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Batch validation</th><td>Each lot validated against reference methods</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Lot records</th><td>Traceability from raw materials to finished-goods release</td></tr>
        <tr><th>QMS</th><td>ISO 13485:2016 (GB/T 42061) quality management system</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Raw material control</th><td>Antibody and membrane qualification before production</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    For buyer qualification, request validation data, certificate of analysis (COA) and stability data —
    see the <a href="/knowledge/procurement-guide/">supplier evaluation checklist</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the internal control line for?</strong> It validates each device, distinguishing a true negative from a failed test.</p>
  </div>

